<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhagat Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Shaheed-e-Azam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhagat Singh was an Indian anti-colonial revolutionary, born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1907, who became a martyr and folk hero in Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> after his execution at the age of 23. A member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a> Socialist Republican Association, in December 1928 he and Shivaram Rajguru shot dead the junior police officer John P. Saunders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, mistaking him for the superintendent James Scott, whom they held responsible for the death of Lala Lajpat Rai. In April 1929 he and Batukeshwar Dutt set off two low-intensity bombs in the Central Legislative Assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and let themselves be arrested, and his hunger strike in jail won wide sympathy. He was convicted of murder and hanged in March 1931.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhagat-singh.jpg" alt="Bhagat Singh" width="330" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lahore_conspiracy_case_poster_9th_Oct_193o_jindal_sunam_12x9_copy_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bhagat Singh was born into a Punjabi Jat Sikh family in the village of Banga, in the Lyallpur district of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. He was the second of seven children of Vidyavati and Kishan Singh Sandhu. His father and his uncle Ajit Singh took part in progressive politics, including the agitation of 1907 around the Canal Colonisation Bill and later the Ghadar Movement. After the village school he attended the Dayanand Anglo-Vedic School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, and in 1923 he joined the National College there, which Lala Lajpat Rai had founded two years earlier in response to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>'s non-cooperation movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the Simon Commission visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> on 30 October 1928, Lajpat Rai led a march against it. The superintendent of police, James A. Scott, ordered a lathi charge and personally assaulted Rai, who died of a heart attack on 17 November. Singh, a prominent member of the Hindustan Republican Association, probably did much to change its name to the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association. On 17 December 1928 he and Shivaram Rajguru shot John P. Saunders, who had left the District Police Headquarters; the postmortem found eight bullet wounds. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandra-shekhar-azad">Chandra Shekhar Azad</a> shot dead Head Constable Channan Singh, who had given chase. Bhagat Singh escaped by train, with his hair cut and wearing Western dress, passing as a married couple with Durgawati Devi.</p><p>In 1929 he proposed a dramatic act to gain publicity: a bomb in the Central Legislative Assembly, nominally against the Public Safety Bill and the Trade Dispute Act. After it was thrown on 8 April, he and Batukeshwar Dutt dropped leaflets and were arrested. In the jail he joined Jatin Das in a hunger strike for better conditions, and Das died of starvation in September 1929.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Singh, Rajguru and Sukhdev were sentenced to death in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> conspiracy case and hanged in the Lahore jail on 23 March 1931, 11 hours ahead of the schedule of 24 March. Their bodies were cremated in secret near Ganda Singh Wala village and the ashes thrown into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>. He had written that he held human life sacred, but that force used for a legitimate cause was morally justified, and he declined to sign a draft letter for clemency.</p><p>An atheist, he read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Bakunin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>, Lenin and Trotsky, and in his last testament named a Marxist basis for social reconstruction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> wrote that he became popular because he seemed to vindicate the honour of Lajpat Rai, and that the act was forgotten while the symbol remained. A bronze statue of him was installed in the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> on 15 August 2008. Communists, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> nationalists and others have each claimed his memory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhagat Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1928 the all-British Simon Commission was boycotted; Lala Lajpat Rai died weeks after being beaten in a police baton charge at Lahore, and <b>Bhagat Singh</b> and his comrades shot dead a young police officer in revenge.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Indians condemned his refusal to protest against the executions of <b>Bhagat Singh</b>, Sukhdev, Udham Singh and Rajguru by the colonial government.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/gandhism">Gandhism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>It has also been associated with the younger generation of freedom fighters between the wars, among them Ashfaqullah Khan, <b>Bhagat Singh</b> and Chandrashekhar Azad.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sarfaroshi-ki-tamanna">Sarfaroshi Ki Tamanna</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.55,74.34&amp;year=1922" aria-label="Lahore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6468 1015 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Bhagat Singh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6918 1285h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6918 1285h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.55,74.34&amp;year=1922">What was here in 1922? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
